---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A candlestick price chart and a rack of stacked coins, reduced to flat gold and black shapes on warm cream, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You'll spend the semester reading real, published data from player-run
    virtual economies --- EVE Online's Monthly Economic Reports, World of
    Warcraft's Auction House, Old School RuneScape's Grand Exchange --- and
    learning the tools their designers use to keep those economies from
    collapsing: faucet-and-sink accounting, cost-effectiveness ratios,
    market microstructure, and mechanism design. You'll build spreadsheets
    that model real ships, real prices, and real scandals, not toy numbers
    invented for the exercise. You walk out with a portfolio of that
    analysis and a capstone report proposing a scoped, numerically justified
    fix to a real problem in a real economy.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Built for students who like systems more than they like any one game ---
    you don't need to have played EVE Online, WoW, or RuneScape, but you do
    need to be comfortable with a spreadsheet and unbothered by a ratio.
    It suits computing and engineering students looking for a systems-design
    elective with real numerical modelling in it, not a games-studies
    seminar. If "market" and "mechanism" sound more interesting to you than
    "lore," you're in the right place.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
